<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ecab</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.) · capital Ecab</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maya province at the north-eastern tip of Yucatán, the first part of mainland Mexico seen by a Spanish expedition in 1517.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ecab (Ekab) emerged after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> around 1441 in the north-east corner of the peninsula, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isla-mujeres">Isla Mujeres</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cozumel">Cozumel</a>. Its main town, Ecab, impressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-de-cordoba-yucatan-conquistador">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba</a> in 1517, who called it '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ecab was a coastal province whose towns lived from fishing, salt and the seaborne trade that passed around the peninsula. Little is known of its rulers or internal organisation, which colonial records describe only briefly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ecab resisted early Spanish incursions but was conquered in the 1540s by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-nephew">Francisco de Montejo the Nephew</a>. Its population declined sharply under colonial rule and the town was later abandoned.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.38%;width:2.05%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1544 CE (approx.)